1
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:05,310
I was eight the summer the Sun went out.

2
00:00:06,530 --> 00:00:08,320
I was drawing the Sun.

3
00:00:09,190 --> 00:00:11,525
Who draws at six in the morning?

4
00:00:11,895 --> 00:00:13,055
MIRA: Artists.

5
00:00:13,895 --> 00:00:16,280
And Biscuit was planning a crime.

6
00:00:16,650 --> 00:00:19,785
At that moment, half the
world was in daylight.

7
00:00:25,155 --> 00:00:26,545
Biscuit, no!

8
00:00:27,155 --> 00:00:28,890
Then the Sun went out.

9
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:34,600
MIRA: Is it night again?

10
00:00:36,360 --> 00:00:38,870
It can't be night. It's breakfast time.

11
00:00:41,310 --> 00:00:44,020
The lights still worked. The Sun didn't.

12
00:00:44,390 --> 00:00:47,000
Then someone knocked. Three times.

13
00:00:47,990 --> 00:00:53,280
MR. ITO: Good morning. Your mother just phoned me
from the hospital. You're with me today.

14
00:00:53,650 --> 00:00:57,085
Put your shoes on, please. Both of you.

15
00:00:57,455 --> 00:01:02,965
Mr. Ito, from upstairs. He was
seventy-three, and he was never in a hurry.

16
00:01:03,335 --> 00:01:07,270
His bag was already packed. It
had been packed for years.

17
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:10,805
MR. ITO: And bring the dog.
He has your toast.

18
00:01:11,175 --> 00:01:13,665
It took me a year to understand why.

19
00:01:14,835 --> 00:01:17,595
Outside, the whole street was looking up.

20
00:01:17,965 --> 00:01:21,200
Mum was a nurse. She was
stuck at the hospital.

21
00:01:21,570 --> 00:01:23,155
It's just an eclipse!

22
00:01:23,570 --> 00:01:28,435
MR. ITO: An eclipse never lasts longer
than seven and a half minutes.

23
00:01:28,805 --> 00:01:31,890
It was happening everywhere,
at the same moment.

24
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:35,145
In New York, it was two in the morning.

25
00:01:35,815 --> 00:01:39,100
Most of them woke up to a
morning that never came.

26
00:01:39,870 --> 00:01:43,510
MR. ITO: Up to the roof. Kai,
bring my telescope.

27
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:49,568
The lift was packed with neighbours, so Kai
carried it up six floors. He complained

28
00:01:49,568 --> 00:01:50,665
about every one.

29
00:01:51,035 --> 00:01:55,295
Mr. Ito came up here every
morning to watch the sunrise.

30
00:01:55,665 --> 00:01:58,200
That morning, he had seen the last one.

31
00:01:58,850 --> 00:02:01,240
MR. ITO: First, let's find Venus.

32
00:02:01,610 --> 00:02:03,770
MIRA: Why is that star still shining?

33
00:02:04,140 --> 00:02:10,286
MR. ITO: That's Venus. It shines with sunlight
bouncing off it, and that light takes a longer

34
00:02:10,286 --> 00:02:11,530
road to reach us.

35
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:18,125
If something has happened to the Sun itself,
Venus will go dark too, at four minutes

36
00:02:18,125 --> 00:02:18,940
past seven.

37
00:02:19,310 --> 00:02:20,700
So we waited.

38
00:02:24,470 --> 00:02:27,610
MR. ITO: Then something has
happened at the Sun.

39
00:02:28,380 --> 00:02:34,270
Jupiter is much further away. Its light
should go out at four minutes past eight.

40
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:37,105
At four minutes past eight, it did.

41
00:02:37,875 --> 00:02:43,090
He tapped the telescope three times. He
always did that. I never asked why.

42
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:57,170
(RADIO) Stay indoors and keep warm. Power
and water are working normally.

43
00:02:57,540 --> 00:02:59,980
Everyone's going to the shops anyway.

44
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:03,240
MR. ITO: Then so are we.

45
00:03:03,750 --> 00:03:07,215
Kai was right. The whole
city had the same idea.

46
00:03:07,585 --> 00:03:10,020
Rice. Tins. Warm socks. Seeds?

47
00:03:10,390 --> 00:03:13,155
MIRA: And dog treats.
They're on Biscuit's list.

48
00:03:13,525 --> 00:03:17,035
Two grown men fought over the
last electric heater.

49
00:03:17,405 --> 00:03:22,720
MR. ITO: Heat won't be the hard part. Food
will. Nothing grows without the Sun.

50
00:03:23,090 --> 00:03:25,130
Kai! Mira! Can you see me?

51
00:03:25,850 --> 00:03:27,565
Mum! Are you okay?

52
00:03:27,935 --> 00:03:31,875
I'm fine, love. But I'm sleeping
here. They need every nurse.

53
00:03:32,245 --> 00:03:35,710
The ambulances haven't
stopped since it went dark.

54
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:37,695
MIRA: When are you coming home?

55
00:03:38,080 --> 00:03:43,220
As soon as I can. I'll call every night
at seven. Be good for Mr. Ito.

56
00:03:43,590 --> 00:03:46,525
And she did call, every single night.

57
00:03:46,895 --> 00:03:49,635
Mr. Ito slept on our sofa.

58
00:03:50,005 --> 00:03:51,770
Is the Sun… dead?

59
00:03:52,440 --> 00:03:57,830
MR. ITO: I don't think so. If it had vanished,
its pull on the sea would vanish too.

60
00:03:58,200 --> 00:04:02,190
And tomorrow's high tide
would be a third lower.

61
00:04:03,310 --> 00:04:08,400
Here's a tide table. Go and
check. High tide is at eleven.

62
00:04:08,770 --> 00:04:12,435
So the next morning, Kai and I
walked down to the harbour.

63
00:04:12,805 --> 00:04:14,470
MIRA: It's right on the line!

64
00:04:14,840 --> 00:04:17,550
So the Sun's still there. We
just can't see it.

65
00:04:17,920 --> 00:04:19,485
MIRA: Then why is it dark?

66
00:04:20,155 --> 00:04:24,015
The Sun hadn't gone anywhere.
Something was in front of it.

67
00:04:24,885 --> 00:04:26,950
MIRA: The fish haven't even noticed!

68
00:04:27,320 --> 00:04:30,910
NEWS ANCHOR: Infrared telescopes have
found something around the Sun.

69
00:04:31,280 --> 00:04:35,295
It's warm. It's round. And it's
twice as wide as the Sun.

70
00:04:35,965 --> 00:04:38,200
Not a cloud. Not a planet.

71
00:04:38,570 --> 00:04:40,885
A shell. Wrapped around a star.

72
00:04:42,055 --> 00:04:45,195
NEWS ANCHOR: Governments are
asking everyone to stay calm.

73
00:04:45,545 --> 00:04:47,185
Nobody was calm.

74
00:04:47,955 --> 00:04:50,065
MIRA: Mr. Ito, who built it?

75
00:04:51,585 --> 00:04:54,000
MR. ITO: It's bedtime, Mira.

76
00:04:54,370 --> 00:04:56,455
It was four in the afternoon.

77
00:04:57,105 --> 00:04:59,595
He'd never sent me to bed before.

78
00:05:00,365 --> 00:05:05,055
NEWS ANCHOR: A solar storm that left the Sun
before it went dark is reaching Earth tonight.

79
00:05:05,425 --> 00:05:07,090
MIRA: It's saying goodbye.

80
00:05:14,260 --> 00:05:17,100
By Day 7, it was twenty below.

81
00:05:17,470 --> 00:05:22,360
The pipes in our building froze and
burst, and the radiators went cold.

82
00:05:22,730 --> 00:05:26,890
All over the world, heating was
never built for cold like this.

83
00:05:27,260 --> 00:05:30,745
Mr. Ito showed us how to
keep one room warm.

84
00:05:31,115 --> 00:05:34,100
That night, Kai pretended
he wasn't scared.

85
00:05:34,470 --> 00:05:36,885
He was holding my hand too hard.

86
00:05:37,755 --> 00:05:39,495
Then we smelled smoke.

87
00:05:39,865 --> 00:05:44,930
MR. ITO: Next door's heating is off too.
They're burning furniture, indoors!

88
00:05:45,300 --> 00:05:46,965
Everyone out!

89
00:05:47,335 --> 00:05:53,850
A fire indoors makes carbon monoxide. You
can't see it or smell it, and it kills.

90
00:05:54,220 --> 00:05:56,805
Mr. Ito got the whole floor out.

91
00:05:57,175 --> 00:05:59,735
At the street door, Biscuit panicked.

92
00:06:00,105 --> 00:06:01,240
MIRA: BISCUIT!

93
00:06:02,610 --> 00:06:04,275
Biscuit! Come on, boy!

94
00:06:04,645 --> 00:06:10,083
The fire brigade put the fire out. Kai
searched all night, and came back without

95
00:06:10,083 --> 00:06:10,355
him.

96
00:06:10,725 --> 00:06:14,490
Mira, listen to me.
Biscuit is a clever dog.

97
00:06:14,860 --> 00:06:17,625
Clever dogs find warm places.

98
00:06:18,395 --> 00:06:20,710
MIRA: Kai, where would it still be warm?

99
00:06:21,060 --> 00:06:23,420
I don't know. But I'm going to find him.

100
00:06:24,090 --> 00:06:26,050
Kai went straight back out.

101
00:06:26,690 --> 00:06:28,775
Paw prints. Fresh ones.

102
00:06:29,145 --> 00:06:34,160
They led to the only warm thing on the
street: air rising from the metro.

103
00:06:36,280 --> 00:06:38,345
You clever, clever dog.

104
00:06:38,715 --> 00:06:43,950
A few metres down, the ground stays at
about ten degrees, Sun or no Sun.

105
00:06:44,320 --> 00:06:47,880
Biscuit hadn't run away. He'd
found the way down.

106
00:06:48,250 --> 00:06:51,835
By morning, half the street had
followed him underground.

107
00:06:52,205 --> 00:06:55,515
All over the world, cities
moved underground.

108
00:06:55,885 --> 00:06:59,670
By Day 30, Harbour Street
station was a town.

109
00:07:00,040 --> 00:07:04,425
The power stations kept running
on coal, gas and nuclear.

110
00:07:04,795 --> 00:07:07,730
Electric trains carried food and fuel.

111
00:07:08,100 --> 00:07:12,860
Anyone who could work from home did.
Everyone else worked underground.

112
00:07:13,230 --> 00:07:18,669
We grew lettuce, because it's ready in a
month. Potatoes. And mushrooms, which don't

113
00:07:18,669 --> 00:07:19,640
need any light.

114
00:07:20,010 --> 00:07:22,620
Everyone got very sick of lettuce.

115
00:07:22,990 --> 00:07:27,375
At seven every morning, the
engineers turned on the big lamps.

116
00:07:27,745 --> 00:07:29,885
MR. ITO: We'll call it sunrise.

117
00:07:31,005 --> 00:07:34,665
MIRA: It's yellow. And round. And
it's warm on your face.

118
00:07:35,035 --> 00:07:38,000
The littlest ones had
started drawing it grey.

119
00:07:38,970 --> 00:07:41,880
I kept my drawing in my pocket, all year.

120
00:07:42,650 --> 00:07:45,815
Biscuit got a fan club.
And a bigger belly.

121
00:07:46,185 --> 00:07:51,595
That month, the hospital opened a clinic in
our station. Mum asked to work there.

122
00:07:51,965 --> 00:07:53,730
And then she was home.

123
00:07:55,365 --> 00:07:57,255
You kept them alive.

124
00:07:57,625 --> 00:07:59,865
MR. ITO: No. They kept me alive.

125
00:08:06,835 --> 00:08:09,070
MIRA: Mr. Ito. Who built it?

126
00:08:09,440 --> 00:08:12,275
Whoever it was, it must
have taken them ages.

127
00:08:12,645 --> 00:08:16,385
MR. ITO: Much longer than
that. It's very old. Here.

128
00:08:17,055 --> 00:08:21,695
Nineteen-nineteen. A total
eclipse. Look at the lines.

129
00:08:22,065 --> 00:08:26,630
It was always there. Edge-on,
like the slats of a blind.

130
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:31,140
On the morning the Sun went
out, every slat turned.

131
00:08:31,910 --> 00:08:34,025
MIRA: You've seen those lines before.

132
00:08:34,395 --> 00:08:38,135
MR. ITO: Once. A long time
ago. Nobody listened.

133
00:08:38,905 --> 00:08:42,865
I believed him. It was true. It
just wasn’t all of it.

134
00:08:43,835 --> 00:08:47,395
On Day 100, it was fifty below up top.

135
00:08:47,765 --> 00:08:51,975
The heaters that warm the air coming
down into the station froze.

136
00:08:52,345 --> 00:08:56,210
MR. ITO: Let me help. I know a
little about old machines.

137
00:08:56,580 --> 00:08:58,540
That was the coldest night.

138
00:08:58,910 --> 00:09:03,095
All over the world, many people did
not make it through that winter.

139
00:09:04,065 --> 00:09:06,130
How does this end, Mira?

140
00:09:07,300 --> 00:09:09,140
MIRA: With the Sun coming back.

141
00:09:10,110 --> 00:09:15,095
By morning, Mr. Ito and the engineers
had the heaters running again.

142
00:09:15,465 --> 00:09:16,925
MIRA: What are you building?

143
00:09:17,465 --> 00:09:20,655
MR. ITO: Something to listen
with. And to answer.

144
00:09:21,425 --> 00:09:26,310
NEWS ANCHOR: After months of searching, astronomers
have found where the Sun's light is going.

145
00:09:26,680 --> 00:09:32,420
One beam, aimed at a star
called HD 219134.

146
00:09:32,790 --> 00:09:36,855
And there's something inside it,
coming toward us and slowing down.

147
00:09:37,625 --> 00:09:39,090
MR. ITO: They're coming.

148
00:09:40,460 --> 00:09:45,554
NEWS ANCHOR: At this rate, it will stop in
sixty-five days. Scientists hope the Sun will come

149
00:09:45,554 --> 00:09:46,700
back when it does.

150
00:09:47,470 --> 00:09:49,355
I didn't ask who he meant.

151
00:09:50,025 --> 00:09:54,610
All the Sun's light, pushing against
one small thing, coming our way.

152
00:09:59,980 --> 00:10:06,020
On Day 365, the escalators to the
surface had frozen solid.

153
00:10:06,390 --> 00:10:11,200
Kai carried Mr. Ito's dish all the way
up. He didn't complain once.

154
00:10:11,570 --> 00:10:13,905
Half the station came up with us.

155
00:10:14,275 --> 00:10:17,915
It was seventy below, so we
wore everything we owned.

156
00:10:18,285 --> 00:10:21,799
MIRA: One whole lap around the
Sun. Happy birthday, Earth.

157
00:10:22,169 --> 00:10:26,909
MR. ITO: It started at 6:52,
exactly one year ago.

158
00:10:27,279 --> 00:10:30,969
I think it stops at 6:52 today.

159
00:10:31,339 --> 00:10:34,854
Listen. Something near the
Sun is calling us.

160
00:10:35,224 --> 00:10:36,009
One.

161
00:10:36,759 --> 00:10:37,294
Two.

162
00:10:38,044 --> 00:10:38,779
Three.

163
00:10:39,849 --> 00:10:41,539
Then it didn't stop.

164
00:10:42,409 --> 00:10:44,769
That's not three. That's hundreds.

165
00:10:45,139 --> 00:10:48,929
MR. ITO: One thousand, six
hundred and seventy-nine.

166
00:10:49,599 --> 00:10:51,564
Why that exact number?

167
00:10:52,134 --> 00:10:56,724
MR. ITO: Because it's twenty-three times
seventy-three, and nothing else.

168
00:10:57,394 --> 00:11:04,209
We used the same trick ourselves in
1974, to send a picture into space.

169
00:11:04,879 --> 00:11:06,869
MIRA: Like squares on graph paper.

170
00:11:07,519 --> 00:11:08,859
It's a picture!

171
00:11:09,729 --> 00:11:13,269
MR. ITO: Twenty-three across.
Seventy-three down.

172
00:11:13,729 --> 00:11:16,744
At the top: counting, from one to ten.

173
00:11:17,114 --> 00:11:20,979
Then one of them. And one of us,
beside them, much smaller.

174
00:11:21,749 --> 00:11:25,134
Their star. And their ship,
parked beside our Sun.

175
00:11:26,104 --> 00:11:28,239
MIRA: Mr. Ito? What is it?

176
00:11:28,609 --> 00:11:30,324
MR. ITO: It's a reply.

177
00:11:31,094 --> 00:11:32,109
MIRA: To who?

178
00:11:33,094 --> 00:11:34,384
MR. ITO: To me.

179
00:11:49,154 --> 00:11:54,514
The young man in the
observatory was Mr. Ito, in 1983.

180
00:11:55,284 --> 00:11:59,974
He found lines in old eclipse
photos. They pointed to one star.

181
00:12:00,344 --> 00:12:05,834
MR. ITO: It was a signpost. It meant: if
you can see this, call us. So I did.

182
00:12:06,204 --> 00:12:12,239
The astronomers he worked for told him not
to. He knocked anyway. One. Two. Three.

183
00:12:13,009 --> 00:12:17,899
MR. ITO: And they counted on, all the way
to ten, so I'd know it was them.

184
00:12:18,269 --> 00:12:23,442
His knock took twenty-one years to reach
them. They set off the day it arrived. It

185
00:12:23,442 --> 00:12:26,029
took them twenty-three years to get here.

186
00:12:26,399 --> 00:12:32,392
MR. ITO: Something that fast can't stop by
itself. It needs a push from the front. Only a

187
00:12:32,392 --> 00:12:34,489
whole star pushes that hard.

188
00:12:34,859 --> 00:12:41,183
They built that shell long ago, as a harbour,
so one day they could stop here. How,

189
00:12:41,183 --> 00:12:42,174
nobody knows.

190
00:12:42,544 --> 00:12:45,984
So for one year, our Sun was their brake.

191
00:12:46,354 --> 00:12:50,769
In a few minutes they'll have stopped,
and they won't need it anymore.

192
00:12:51,139 --> 00:12:55,954
I knew they might come any year now.
That's why my bag was packed.

193
00:12:56,724 --> 00:12:58,384
MIRA: Why didn't they warn us?

194
00:12:58,754 --> 00:13:02,644
MR. ITO: Because a warning can't
travel faster than light.

195
00:13:03,214 --> 00:13:07,379
They were almost that fast. It
would have arrived with them.

196
00:13:08,149 --> 00:13:09,984
MIRA: Did they know it would hurt us?

197
00:13:10,754 --> 00:13:12,269
MR. ITO: I don't know.

198
00:13:13,439 --> 00:13:15,604
I'm sorry, Mira.

199
00:13:16,974 --> 00:13:20,309
At the bottom of their picture
was an empty square.

200
00:13:21,079 --> 00:13:22,419
MIRA: It's our turn.

201
00:13:23,389 --> 00:13:27,124
Every radio telescope on Earth
got the same picture.

202
00:13:27,694 --> 00:13:31,304
Every government started arguing
about what to say back.

203
00:13:33,024 --> 00:13:34,534
MIRA: Can we send this?

204
00:13:34,904 --> 00:13:37,119
MR. ITO: Yes. I think we can.

205
00:13:37,889 --> 00:13:40,349
My drawing from the morning it went out.

206
00:13:40,699 --> 00:13:44,259
A big yellow sun. And me,
standing under it.

207
00:13:45,129 --> 00:13:47,414
Are we even allowed to do this?

208
00:13:47,764 --> 00:13:49,479
MR. ITO: Probably not.

209
00:13:50,449 --> 00:13:54,384
He pressed the key, the
way he had in 1983.

210
00:13:54,754 --> 00:13:56,444
Five minutes to go.

211
00:13:57,214 --> 00:14:00,624
The whole world was watching
the same patch of sky.

212
00:14:00,994 --> 00:14:02,409
MIRA: What if he's wrong?

213
00:14:02,759 --> 00:14:04,724
Then we'll wait together.

214
00:14:14,594 --> 00:14:15,854
MIRA: There it is!

215
00:14:17,024 --> 00:14:21,234
Everywhere it was daytime, the Sun
came back at the same moment.

216
00:14:27,804 --> 00:14:33,165
In New York it was the middle of the night.
They got their sunrise a few hours

217
00:14:33,165 --> 00:14:34,814
later, the ordinary way.

218
00:14:35,584 --> 00:14:41,494
At 6:52, exactly one year after it went
out, we were standing in sunlight.

219
00:14:42,864 --> 00:14:47,749
Every morning for forty-three years,
Mr. Ito watched the sunrise.

220
00:14:48,719 --> 00:14:51,754
I used to think it was
because he liked it.

221
00:14:52,924 --> 00:14:55,014
He was waiting for an answer.

222
00:14:56,584 --> 00:15:00,769
Sixteen minutes after we sent my
drawing, the dish clicked again.

223
00:15:03,984 --> 00:15:06,844
What would you have sent?
Tell us in the comments.
